nna fit in sync  with each other when when those two  things are related  so  that's why i think i'm not that great of  it because like i don't always know what  direction  like what consistent path to take from  an advice standpoint  talk to me about the touch points with  your executive team right so as you're  running the business uh do you meet with  them  once a day once a week once a month like  like what does that kind of cadence look  like and i think this is more uh style  right as each business leader thinks  about it differently some people want to  hey i want to talk to you every single  day other people say look we've got one  big meeting on wednesday for three hours  all the executives come together and we  kind of review the business how do you  work so we've gone through multiple  different iterations of this like we've  had executives we've lost executives  we've  worked without executives  i don't actually believe that again like  early stage companies like slightly  post-product market fit work with like  seasoned executive teams if you're  pulling seasoned leaders out of you know  big companies and expecting them to work  well in startups you know it's a lost  cause it's not gonna work um  and the structure that those people  typically type to like to type  try and work in um don't work too well  for startups what we did was we did  weekly leadership meetings on wednesday  it was two hours we would have everyone  put together a memo the night before  we'd have a general memo you know and  what happens is everyone gives their  opinion on every different thing and  it's not clear is this the  decision-making meeting are there how  many different decision makers are there  everyone wants to feel like their input  is heard on a particular decision  everyone wants to feel like an owner of  the decision and you as the founder the  person that's running the meeting is  like going crazy because everyone wants  to have their input on the thing and  certain people have different levels of  impact on a particular decision but if  they're all in the meeting their voices  are all relatively equal right so if  you're thinking about a product decision  and your chief people officer is  weighing in a ton on the product  decision and she's the loudest voice in  the room and your head of product isn't  really talking too much right which is  something that commonly happens in a  leadership meeting you know you're  diluting that meeting right so you kind  of need to either tell people like hey  like you know stop talking and like you  know this person's like how do you  handle it you literally just right in  the middle of the meeting say hey you  know be quiet  so  yeah i started to do that in the  beginning i was very polite um  what i actually think works in the right  structure doing this  is  leadership teams don't work as big teams  like you have your team of you have your  group of functional leaders  so this could be five to seven people  and then you have your s team and your s  team maybe it's your like cto maybe it's  your finance leader maybe it's your  co-founder it can't be any more than  like three or four people it's gonna be  a small group and you have to have a lot  of like respect and trust in that group  and you were talking to this team every  single day like they are helping you run  the whole business and they are filled  in they are keyed in on everything  this team will help you make the  decisions this team is the  decision-making body then you have the  information body and the information  body is all the different functional  executive leaders  they might run the people function they  might run the legal function whatever it  might be  and they will help  you keep them informed you do your  two-hour weekly leadership meeting and  you will keep them informed as to what  is going on you will get their  perspective but the decisions are not  made in that meeting the decisions are  not made in that meeting  and do you make the decisions before  the meeting  that it's kind of you're making them all  the time okay but you're clearly logging  those decisions you're logging who's the  dri who's responsible for this  particular decision you have to be very  clear you have a leadership operating  system so in our case we had a  leadership operating system this is how  we make decisions this is who how we  appoint dris this is what a dri is  responsible for it's a dra a directly  responsible individual  we have aors which was really helpful so  aor's are areas of responsibility we  basically had a big spreadsheet in early  stage companies people don't know who's  responsible for what you literally write  out to the specific thing what is the  area of responsibility who is the owner  of the aor  what are they specifically responsible  for what is the backup  so who will in the case they're not  there who's the person you go to and  then what is the process write up how do  you go through this actual process so  the way it works is when someone quits  when someone leaves when someone's out  it's really easy for people to swap in  and take over transition aors  and when you do all of this how much are  you having to train employees on it when  they first show up so a brand new  employee and you're saying hey here's  how we run meetings here's how we do  information sessions here's who the  leadership team is let's actually make  the decision and solve that versus it's  more of uh hey you are an engineer you  are a marketing person you are on the  customer service team  here's what you're going to need to know  on a day-to-day basis and then they'll  kind of over time uh through osmosis  essentially just pick up  okay this is kind of how the  organization works so we did the latter  but moving forward  i think having a formal like week-long  training program even if it only works  one percent of the time that one percent  will continue to compound um and  hopefully it works much more than that  um to how here's how the company  operates here's our culture here's how  we view our values um  here's how we make decisions all of  those different things  and deeply embedding it like almost  having an onboarding class and making  sure people get it right  is really critical and the other part to  culture is like how do you hold people  accountable when they don't do things  like that how do you do it  we're still figuring out the specific  the right way to doing it but it's  usually rewarding people when they do so  we have channel we have slack channels  where we call people out for positively  doing it and then i always give people  feedback like hey i would have liked you  to see this differently in a one-on-one  setting like never criticize people in  public  and how often do you have those feedback  sessions or those one-on-ones are you  doing that uh multiple times a day all  week long with various people in the  company are you doing it just with the  leadership team so previously we did  adjust with the leadership team and we  did two things  um as far as feedback so we did feedback  at the end of every meeting so people  would write down their feedback they  would say okay here's how we thought the  meeting went we'd like carve out 10 to  15 minutes at the end of the meeting  here's how the meeting went here's some  of the learnings here's what i would do  differently next time and everyone would  write it down and we would review it as  a team and then we would put a next  action of like next week we're going to  do this better and then we'd always make  sure to remember that as a team after  every meeting the second thing we did  was on one-on-ones i would do  basically check-ins so i would ask  people i could ask people for specific  like questions about how they would rate  like working with us so how do you feel  our working relationship is this week is  it a two is it a five is it an eight is  it a ten if it's less than eight what  would make it an eight or higher and you  would score it and you would do this  like once a month or once every two  weeks and you would see the scores  progressively change and get better over  time  and  when you saw the score  low  is that your problem or their problem  i mean  sometimes both you have to understand  like what is the component part like why  um  sometimes it is your problem sometimes  it is their problem and then you work  out a plan like what is the next action  what are we going to do differently  the last question i have for you is uh  after running the business for six years  if you go back and uh there's plenty of  young founders who are starting  companies today  what's like the one or two things that  you wish that you knew and you would  really hammer home  so there's a lot of distractions  that is really important to avoid and i  think there's more distractions now than  there was in  20  2015 like whenever i was starting and i  think avoiding those distractions is  really important there's only one thing  that really matters  and like it's your customers and your  product as a result so maybe two things  um  other than that like product and  customers  and like maybe like you can kind of  start to think about like okay so once  you nail product and customers and  you've got culture and you know some of  these other things to running a company  um  nothing none of that other stuff matters  right founders get too obsessed  especially young founders with status  right i fell into this trap i personally  like it just seemed like what everyone  else was talking about and it seemed  like the important thing if you can't  nail customers in product you're going  to fail at everything else  like  the types of vcs you raise money from  the pedigree of the employees you  recruit the  time that you spend on going to founder  networking events all of it it's a waste  of time that i 100 agree it doesn't feel  like that in the beginning right but  over time i think that you realize that  i'm sure you had a ton of impact on  making this together but like  yeah and i'm a huge fan of keith or boy  but um like the miami tech week thing  like half of our employees were like oh  like we want to go to this miami tech  week thing and i'm like look like  the vcs that are like retired and like  want to hang out and  like they can go to miami tech week he's  gonna kill you for saying retired  they can maybe not keith but because  keith has a broader mission which is  trying to get people to miami but  keith aside like  you need to stay focused on the core  objective which is our customers and our  product and anything else that isn't  that miami tech week is a distraction if  you're going for vacation go on vacation  but like all of this other stuff if it's  not vacation it is a distraction  i think look it's just singular focus on  what you feel is the most important  thing right i think it's a pretty fair  uh uh way to look at a business and i  actually think it's a very good way to  look at a business and for those that uh  end up um  uh not liking that like they can work a  different business yeah exactly right  where can we send people to find you on  the internet or find out more about vice  advise.com  and  vi vise.com and i guess i'm on twitter i  haven't been tweeting much but maybe  i'll start again well if i can find the  kanye tweet that means you definitely  gotta start tweeting more exactly  awesome well listen i really appreciate  this i think people will learn a ton not  only about artificial intelligence  machine learning but also what you guys  are building and then how you actually  run the business uh and we'll definitely  have to do it again in the future cool  thank you so much for having me on
 

